,1,1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3642d7fd-0113-5cbf-a61e-17061219f8d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446240 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36306" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446241 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,4⟩-⟨-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,3⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}⟨4,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)⟩ \\\\ ∇g(-1,1) & =⟨4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{′}(-1,1) & =∇g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨-8,4⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130811" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function $f(x,y)=2\\sqrt{xy}.$ What is the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction in which the function decreases most rapidly?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "72e3df1a-6338-564c-adc2-85283b26953d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446243 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the opposite direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nIn our case, we compute $-∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(8,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇f(x,y) & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\sqrt{xy})i-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\sqrt{xy})j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{x}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y}}\\,j \\\\ -∇f(8,2) & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{8}}\\,i-\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the rate of change of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(8,2)$ in the direction of $-∇f(8,2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖-∇f(8,2)‖ & =‖-\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-2\\,j‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,2,-1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9974a87-21df-58c9-86ba-41826947b876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446247 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2}y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y{e}^{xy},\\,x{e}^{xy}+3{z}^{2},\\,6yz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,2,-1) & =⟨2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2},\\,2\\cdot {e}^{2\\cdot 2}+3(-1)^{2},\\,6\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{4}+3,\\,-12⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186501247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186501275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186501277 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186501291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1045 "Directional Derivatives" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186501275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500458 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0a41176c-b39d-554a-935e-72997f04c98e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218650048