17592186517501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186517783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186517783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518094 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa6bc3a1-b754-5de6-a15a-17b5690dd1bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518218 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186518044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518218 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518218 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518218 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1,2)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518218 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f39e417c-6152-5bd7-a05f-5fd83a9d6cdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518218 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3}),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}-{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},\\,-3{y}^{2},\\,2xz⟩ \\\\ ∇g(0,1,2) & =⟨{2}^{2},-3(1)^{2},2(0)(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨4,-3,0⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
